Q1. HTTP Request–Response Cycle

The HTTP Request–Response Cycle is the process through which a client, usually a web browser, communicates with a web server to get a web resource such as an HTML page, image, CSS file, or JavaScript file.

        CLIENT (Browser)
               |
               | 1. HTTP Request
               ↓
        Web Server
               |
               | 2. Processes Request
               |
               | 3. HTTP Response
               ↓
        CLIENT (Browser)
               |
               | 4. Displays Resource
               ↓
          Web Page
Major Steps

1. User sends a request:
The process starts when a user enters a URL in the browser or clicks a link. The browser creates an HTTP request for the required resource.

2. Request is sent to the server:
The browser sends the request to the appropriate web server. The request contains information such as the HTTP method, URL, headers, and sometimes data.

3. Server processes the request:
The web server receives the request and determines what resource or information is required. It processes the request and prepares the required response.

4. Server sends a response:
The server sends an HTTP response back to the browser. It usually contains a status code, headers, and the requested content.

5. Browser displays the response:
The browser receives the response and uses the content to display the webpage. If additional CSS, JavaScript, images, or other resources are needed, the browser may send more requests.

Example

When we enter www.example.com in a browser, the browser sends a request to the server. The server processes it and sends back the webpage files. The browser then uses these files to display the webpage.

Q2. Introduction to Web Technologies

The main web technologies used in frontend development are HTML, CSS, JavaScript, Bootstrap, jQuery, and React.js. The assignment asks for their purpose and key features.

1. HTML

HTML (HyperText Markup Language) is used to create the basic structure of a webpage.

Key features:

Defines headings, paragraphs, images, links, tables, and forms.
Uses tags and elements to organize content.
Provides the structure of a webpage.
Supports semantic elements such as <header>, <nav>, and <footer>.

Example:

<h1>My Website</h1>
<p>Welcome to my webpage.</p>
2. CSS

CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) is used to style and design HTML webpages.

Key features:

Changes colors, fonts, and backgrounds.
Controls spacing and positioning.
Helps create responsive webpages.
Can create layouts using Flexbox and Grid.

Example:

h1 {
    color: blue;
    text-align: center;
}
3. JavaScript

JavaScript is a programming language used to add functionality and interactivity to webpages.

Key features:

Makes webpages interactive.
Can respond to user actions such as clicks.
Can change HTML and CSS dynamically.
Can perform calculations and validation.

Example:

alert("Welcome to my website!");
4. Bootstrap

Bootstrap is a frontend framework that provides ready-made CSS classes and components for developing websites quickly.

Key features:

Provides responsive design.
Contains ready-made buttons, cards, navigation bars, forms, etc.
Uses a grid system for creating layouts.
Reduces the amount of CSS that needs to be written.

Example:

<button class="btn btn-primary">Click Me</button>
5. jQuery

jQuery is a JavaScript library that makes many common JavaScript tasks easier and shorter.

Key features:

Simplifies DOM manipulation.
Makes event handling easier.
Provides simple methods for animations.
Simplifies AJAX requests.
Uses shorter syntax compared to traditional JavaScript for many tasks.

Example:

$("#demo").hide();
6. React.js

React.js is a JavaScript library used for building user interfaces, especially for modern web applications.

Key features:

Uses reusable components.
Makes it easier to manage dynamic interfaces.
Uses a virtual DOM for efficient updates.
Helps in developing single-page applications.

Example:

function Welcome() {
    return <h1>Hello World!</h1>;
}
Q3. Client-Side vs. Server-Side Technologies

Client-side and server-side technologies are two important parts of web development.

Client-Side Technologies

Client-side technologies work mainly on the user's browser. The browser receives the required files from the server and executes the client-side code.

Examples:

HTML
CSS
JavaScript
React.js
Bootstrap

Uses:

Designing the webpage.
Creating animations and interactions.
Validating some user inputs.
Updating webpage content without always loading a new page.
Server-Side Technologies

Server-side technologies work on the web server. They process requests, perform calculations, communicate with databases, and send results back to the browser.

Examples:

Node.js
PHP
Python
Java
ASP.NET
Difference
Client-Side	Server-Side
Runs mainly in the browser	Runs on the web server
Focuses on user interface and interaction	Focuses on processing and data
Code can be visible to the user	Code is generally not sent to the browser
Uses HTML, CSS and JavaScript	Uses PHP, Python, Java, Node.js, etc.
Can provide immediate interaction	Handles database and server operations
Example

When a user fills out a login form, HTML/CSS/JavaScript can create and validate the form on the client side. The login information can then be sent to a server-side program, which checks the information against a database.

Q4. Block-Level Elements in HTML

Block-level elements are HTML elements that normally start on a new line and occupy the available width of their parent element.

Characteristics
They normally begin on a new line.
They generally take up the available width.
They can contain other elements depending on the HTML rules.
They are useful for creating the main structure and sections of a webpage.
Examples

1. <div>

The <div> element is commonly used as a container for grouping other elements.

<div>
    <h2>About Me</h2>
    <p>This is my introduction.</p>
</div>

2. <p>

The <p> element is used to create a paragraph.

<p>This is a paragraph.</p>

3. <h1>

The <h1> element is used for the main heading of a webpage.

<h1>My Website</h1>

Other common block-level elements include <section>, <article>, <header>, <footer>, and <ul>.

Q5. Semantic HTML Elements

Semantic HTML elements are elements whose names clearly describe the meaning and purpose of their content.

For example, instead of using only <div> for everything, we can use <header> for the header section and <footer> for the footer section.

Examples
<header>
    <h1>My Website</h1>
</header>

<nav>
    <a href="#">Home</a>
    <a href="#">About</a>
</nav>

<main>
    <section>
        <h2>About Us</h2>
        <p>This is the main content.</p>
    </section>
</main>

<footer>
    <p>Copyright 2026</p>
</footer>
Common Semantic Elements
<header> – represents the header of a page or section.
<nav> – represents navigation links.
<main> – contains the main content.
<section> – represents a particular section of content.
<article> – represents independent content.
<aside> – represents related or side content.
<footer> – represents the footer of a page or section.
Importance

Semantic elements are important because:

They make HTML easier to understand.
They improve the structure of a webpage.
They help search engines understand the content.
They improve accessibility for users using screen readers.
They make the code easier for developers to maintain.

Q6. Creating a Table Using HTML

The required table contains columns for Sr. No., Name, Designation, and Department.

HTML Code
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Employee Table</title>
</head>
<body>

<table border="1">
    <tr>
        <th>Sr. No.</th>
        <th>Name</th>
        <th>Designation</th>
        <th>Department</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>1</td>
